1
00:00:00,720 --> 00:00:06,160
This is the second part of a two-part module covering radiographic anatomy of the chest.

2
00:00:07,800 --> 00:00:15,700
Following completion of this unit, we will have covered all of the normal anatomy on both the frontal and the lateral chest radiographs.

3
00:00:16,300 --> 00:00:23,080
In this unit, we're going to talk about distinguishing the right and left ribs as well as the diaphragms on the lateral view.

4
00:00:23,080 --> 00:00:29,540
And we're going to talk about localization of lung parenchymal abnormalities such as pneumonia and nodules.

5
00:00:32,210 --> 00:00:37,670
We're going to pick up where we left off of the first module, talking about the lateral view of the chest.

6
00:00:38,130 --> 00:00:45,150
In that module, we discussed the normal mediastinal borders and major airways that are identifiable on the lateral view.

7
00:00:45,830 --> 00:00:50,730
Now we're going to talk about three spaces which should be evaluated on this view.

8
00:00:51,630 --> 00:00:55,930
We're going to begin with the retrotracheal triangle or rater triangle.

9
00:00:56,670 --> 00:00:59,510
So let's start by identifying the trachea itself.

10
00:00:59,510 --> 00:01:03,570
Remember, there's the posterior tracheal stripe.

11
00:01:04,470 --> 00:01:09,290
This is going to form the anterior border of the retrotracheal triangle.

12
00:01:10,290 --> 00:01:14,410
The caudal margin is formed by the aortic arch,

13
00:01:15,450 --> 00:01:19,370
the posterior margin by the anterior aspect of the vertebral bodies,

14
00:01:19,370 --> 00:01:22,970
and the superior margin by the thoracic inlet.

15
00:01:24,330 --> 00:01:30,310
So these make up the retrotracheal clear space or triangle, also called the rater triangle.

16
00:01:30,310 --> 00:01:38,870
We'll next move on to the clear space behind the sternum, termed the retrotracheal clear space.

17
00:01:38,870 --> 00:01:41,550
This should be a lucent area in the adult.

18
00:01:42,350 --> 00:01:46,670
In the child, you will have opacity at this site, secondary to the thymus.

19
00:01:46,670 --> 00:01:55,010
And then finally, we're going to have a third clear space behind the heart, termed the retrocardiac clear space.

20
00:01:57,050 --> 00:02:02,770
Another normal finding on a lateral view of the chest is the increasing radiolucency

21
00:02:02,770 --> 00:02:07,370
as you move from cranial to caudal along the vertebral bodies.

22
00:02:07,370 --> 00:02:13,750
So if you think about it, at the lung apices, you have relatively less lung parenchyma

23
00:02:13,750 --> 00:02:18,650
and more overlying soft tissues than you do at the lung basis.

24
00:02:18,770 --> 00:02:25,950
So as you go from cranial to caudal along the spine, you should become more and more lucent.

25
00:02:26,550 --> 00:02:36,330
So if we look at examples here, let's take the relative opacity of each of these representative areas over the spine,

26
00:02:36,330 --> 00:02:44,750
and you can see that as we go from cranial to caudal, the overlying tissue lucency increases.

27
00:02:45,270 --> 00:02:53,810
So this is a normal finding, and the loss of this normal gradation is usually indicative of increased opacity

28
00:02:53,810 --> 00:02:57,830
in the lower lobes, often from airspace disease or a mass.

29
00:02:59,090 --> 00:03:05,550
To round out our discussion of the normal anatomy on the lateral view, we need to talk about bones and soft tissues.

30
00:03:07,330 --> 00:03:12,190
Anteriorly, we're going to have the sternum, so the sternum is best identified on this lateral view.

31
00:03:13,870 --> 00:03:18,350
It's important not to mistake the scapula as pathology.

32
00:03:18,530 --> 00:03:24,850
These are going to be identified as vertically oriented radio densities seen edge-on.

33
00:03:26,330 --> 00:03:30,930
The spine is going to be well identified on the lateral view, particularly the vertebral bodies.

34
00:03:30,930 --> 00:03:37,430
If you'll notice the ribs at each level, one rib appears larger than the other.

35
00:03:37,650 --> 00:03:39,990
This is secondary to radiographic magnification.

36
00:03:40,690 --> 00:03:42,770
We're going to talk about this on the next slide.

37
00:03:42,770 --> 00:03:47,650
But the rib at this level here is larger than the adjacent rib.

38
00:03:49,210 --> 00:03:54,010
The larger rib is the right rib, and the smaller rib is the left rib.

39
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:01,600
We're going to have the right and left portions of the diaphragm, which we'll discuss in the next slide as well,

40
00:04:01,940 --> 00:04:05,760
being able to distinguish right and left portions of the diaphragm.

41
00:04:05,760 --> 00:04:14,640
And then finally, as you follow the diaphragm posteriorly, you'll intersect with the ribs and chest wall.

42
00:04:15,180 --> 00:04:18,040
That's going to be our posterior costophrenic sulcus.

43
00:04:18,580 --> 00:04:24,360
And similar to the frontal view, where we're looking at the lateral costophrenic sulcus and make sure that this is maintained and sharp,

44
00:04:24,760 --> 00:04:29,240
we're going to do the same thing on the lateral view in the posterior costophrenic sulcus.

45
00:04:29,240 --> 00:04:36,040
So how are we going to distinguish right and left ribs and right and left portions of the diaphragm?

46
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:40,520
Well, it's first important to understand the technique.

47
00:04:40,740 --> 00:04:47,260
So lateral images are typically taken with the left side of the patient's body against the detector.

48
00:04:47,880 --> 00:04:56,520
And this is so that the heart is closer to the detector to minimize radiographic magnification of the heart.

49
00:04:56,520 --> 00:05:04,860
So if you think about this, you'll realize that the right side is further away from the detector.

50
00:05:05,160 --> 00:05:07,620
And so everything on the right side will be magnified.

51
00:05:07,960 --> 00:05:13,020
So the right-sided ribs will be relatively magnified when compared to the left

52
00:05:13,020 --> 00:05:20,080
and will project slightly more posteriorly than the left, even if the patient is perfectly positioned laterally.

53
00:05:20,080 --> 00:05:27,000
So if we notice the larger ribs here, that's going to be the right side of the ribs,

54
00:05:27,040 --> 00:05:30,840
and we can follow that rib down to where it intersects the diaphragm.

55
00:05:30,940 --> 00:05:36,900
So in this case, we know that that's the right diaphragm, and this would be the right posterior costophrenic sulcus.

56
00:05:41,280 --> 00:05:46,620
Now if we compare the same level, notice the left-sided rib is smaller because it's closer to the detector

57
00:05:46,620 --> 00:05:48,740
and has less magnification.

58
00:05:50,950 --> 00:05:56,490
If we follow that down, that will intersect with the left diaphragm.

59
00:05:57,670 --> 00:06:01,930
And then we're going to have here as outlined the left posterior costophrenic sulcus.

60
00:06:08,660 --> 00:06:14,940
So one way to distinguish the right and left segments of the diaphragm is simply to identify the ribs

61
00:06:14,940 --> 00:06:21,100
and then follow the ribs caudally to where they intersect with their respective diaphragmes.

62
00:06:23,750 --> 00:06:29,810
Distinguishing the right and left segments of the diaphragm can be difficult on the lateral view.

63
00:06:30,490 --> 00:06:36,810
In the previous example, I talked about using the difference in magnification of the ribs

64
00:06:36,810 --> 00:06:44,170
so we can identify the right ribs as being more posterior and relatively larger than the left-sided ribs.

65
00:06:44,170 --> 00:06:50,250
We can then use this to follow that portion of the lung down to the diaphragm,

66
00:06:50,350 --> 00:06:55,570
and the diaphragm which intersects at those ribs would represent the corresponding diaphragm.

67
00:06:56,290 --> 00:07:02,310
You can also sometimes follow the right hemidiaphragm all the way to the chest wall,

68
00:07:02,610 --> 00:07:07,570
whereas the left hemidiaphragm can be obscured anteriorly by the heart.

69
00:07:08,110 --> 00:07:13,170
So if you see two diaphragms, one goes all the way to the anterior chest wall

70
00:07:13,170 --> 00:07:16,290
where the other one is obscured anteriorly.

71
00:07:16,410 --> 00:07:20,970
The one that's obscured is often the left diaphragm because of the presence of the heart.

72
00:07:21,990 --> 00:07:26,450
Sometimes on the frontal view, you'll be able to identify the stomach bubble on the left side

73
00:07:26,450 --> 00:07:29,670
or possibly interposed colon on the right side.

74
00:07:30,290 --> 00:07:34,150
And then you can take that and use it on the lateral view to help you determine

75
00:07:34,150 --> 00:07:36,170
which is the corresponding diaphragm.

76
00:07:36,170 --> 00:07:41,950
And then sometimes it's clear that one diaphragm is higher than the other on the frontal view,

77
00:07:42,250 --> 00:07:48,810
and then you can use this to help you on the lateral view to identify which diaphragm would correspond

78
00:07:48,810 --> 00:07:51,170
to that higher diaphragm on the lateral view.

79
00:07:52,650 --> 00:07:57,690
So to summarize, sometimes distinguishing right and left segments of the diaphragm can be difficult,

80
00:07:57,690 --> 00:08:02,990
but these are four ways to try to help you in this distinction.

81
00:08:09,820 --> 00:08:13,320
The rater triangle is also known as the retrotracheal triangle.

82
00:08:13,780 --> 00:08:20,300
So as its name implies, it is that which is posterior to the trachea.

83
00:08:20,700 --> 00:08:24,780
The anterior border of this triangle will be the posterior tracheal stripe.

84
00:08:25,500 --> 00:08:28,900
The posterior border is going to be the vertebral body anterior margin.

85
00:08:28,900 --> 00:08:37,440
Cranially, it's going to be bordered by the thoracic inlet and then caudally by the aortic arch.

86
00:08:39,680 --> 00:08:44,640
Remember on the lateral view, you also want to be able to identify the retrosternal

87
00:08:44,640 --> 00:08:46,340
and retrocardiac clear spaces.

88
00:08:46,900 --> 00:08:52,480
We will now turn our attention to the radiographic anatomy of the lungs,

89
00:08:53,000 --> 00:08:56,920
again using cross-sectional imaging as a guide to radiographic findings.

90
00:08:56,920 --> 00:09:02,180
Here we have sagittal reconstructed views of both the right and left lungs.

91
00:09:02,760 --> 00:09:07,740
To get your orientation, the asterisk mark the anterior portion of the chest wall.

92
00:09:08,200 --> 00:09:12,380
This is a female patient, and the asterisk mark the breast tissue.

93
00:09:13,080 --> 00:09:18,140
So we're going to begin on the left-sided image, which represents the right lung.

94
00:09:18,140 --> 00:09:27,120
We're going to have the oblique fissure, or major fissure, which will separate the right lower lobe posteriorly

95
00:09:27,120 --> 00:09:29,820
from the right upper and middle lobes anteriorly.

96
00:09:30,220 --> 00:09:35,700
We're going to have the minor or horizontal fissure extending anteriorly from the major fissure

97
00:09:35,700 --> 00:09:39,860
to contact the anterior pleura, as we see here.

98
00:09:39,860 --> 00:09:51,140
Now remember, fissures are simply folds of the visceral pleura and are identified on CT as lines or relative avascular planes

99
00:09:51,140 --> 00:09:54,420
because the vessels do not cross the fissures.

100
00:09:55,380 --> 00:09:59,720
So on the right, we have both the major and minor fissures.

101
00:10:00,160 --> 00:10:02,700
We're going to have the right lower lobe posteriorly,

102
00:10:02,700 --> 00:10:10,060
the right upper lobe anterior to the major fissure and above the minor fissure,

103
00:10:10,380 --> 00:10:15,580
and the middle lobe anterior to the major fissure and below the minor fissure.

104
00:10:16,360 --> 00:10:19,300
On the left side, the anatomy is a little more straightforward.

105
00:10:19,700 --> 00:10:22,800
We don't have a minor or horizontal fissure.

106
00:10:23,360 --> 00:10:29,260
We're going to have the oblique fissure or major fissure separating the left lower lobe posteriorly

107
00:10:29,260 --> 00:10:32,120
from the left upper lobe anteriorly.

108
00:10:32,700 --> 00:10:38,100
With the left-sided middle lobe equivalent, which is adjacent to the left heart border,

109
00:10:38,680 --> 00:10:42,520
is the lingular segment of the left upper lobe.

110
00:10:44,590 --> 00:10:46,470
I want to begin with the right lung,

111
00:10:46,850 --> 00:10:51,510
and I'm going to sketch out the approximate position of the lung lobes and the fissures.

112
00:10:52,550 --> 00:10:54,770
We're going to start with the right lower lobe,

113
00:10:54,770 --> 00:10:58,130
which extends cranially to about the level of the aortic arch,

114
00:10:58,230 --> 00:11:01,470
as we see here on the frontal view and then the lateral view.

115
00:11:01,470 --> 00:11:07,970
The right upper lobe extends to the level of the apex of the right lung.

116
00:11:08,410 --> 00:11:11,550
Here we see on the frontal view, followed by the lateral view.

117
00:11:12,650 --> 00:11:19,830
And then finally, the middle lobe on the frontal view and on the lateral view.

118
00:11:19,830 --> 00:11:26,950
And note that this is the right-sided lung lobe that abuts the right atrium or the right heart border.

119
00:11:28,190 --> 00:11:33,770
A couple of other things that are worth noting is that there's an extensive overlap of the lobes on the frontal view.

120
00:11:34,330 --> 00:11:40,190
This is going to be important when we talk about localizing parenchymal abnormalities, such as nodules.

121
00:11:40,190 --> 00:11:47,430
As we talked about, the lower lobes extend quite high into the thorax to about the level of the aortic arch.

122
00:11:48,550 --> 00:11:51,090
And then finally, let's talk about the position of the fissures.

123
00:11:51,590 --> 00:11:57,070
We're going to have the approximate position of the minor fissure there on the frontal and lateral views.

124
00:11:57,850 --> 00:12:00,470
Note that given the orientation of the minor fissure,

125
00:12:01,010 --> 00:12:05,250
this is the one that can typically be identified on both views.

126
00:12:05,250 --> 00:12:10,510
Given the orientation of the oblique fissure, as we see here on the lateral view,

127
00:12:11,090 --> 00:12:13,230
we may be able to see the fissure on the lateral view,

128
00:12:13,310 --> 00:12:16,330
but we're not going to be able to see it on the frontal view,

129
00:12:16,430 --> 00:12:19,590
given its orientation relative to the x-ray beam.

130
00:12:21,090 --> 00:12:25,490
Moving on to the left lung, we're going to have the left lower lobe.

131
00:12:26,190 --> 00:12:31,330
Similar to the right, the left lower lobe extends about as high as the aortic arch

132
00:12:31,330 --> 00:12:35,450
on the frontal and lateral views, as we see here.

133
00:12:36,250 --> 00:12:41,670
We're going to have the left upper lobe on the frontal and then the lateral view there.

134
00:12:42,810 --> 00:12:47,530
Notice that the left upper lobe is the lobe which will abut the heart,

135
00:12:48,090 --> 00:12:55,430
and the segment of the left upper lobe that abuts the left heart border is termed the lingula,

136
00:12:55,430 --> 00:12:58,630
which is essentially the middle lobe equivalent on the left.

137
00:12:58,630 --> 00:13:05,570
Again, notice the fairly significant amount of overlap on the frontal view

138
00:13:05,570 --> 00:13:09,590
between the left upper and left lower lobes.

139
00:13:11,410 --> 00:13:15,090
And then finally, notice the position of the oblique fissure.

140
00:13:15,870 --> 00:13:22,030
Again, given its orientation, will only rarely be identified on the frontal view,

141
00:13:22,610 --> 00:13:25,590
but may be identified on the lateral view.

142
00:13:25,590 --> 00:13:32,030
So the question then becomes, how does this radiographic anatomy affect our interpretation

143
00:13:32,030 --> 00:13:37,090
in terms of localizing parenchymal abnormalities such as pulmonary nodules?

144
00:13:37,610 --> 00:13:41,650
Well, again, remember there's extensive overlap of these lobes on the frontal view.

145
00:13:42,170 --> 00:13:44,770
So, for instance, if we have a nodule at this position,

146
00:13:45,550 --> 00:13:49,310
if an overlap, we're not really sure on the frontal view alone

147
00:13:49,310 --> 00:13:54,350
if this is located in the right upper lobe or the right lower lobe.

148
00:13:55,010 --> 00:13:59,530
So, a general radiographic principle is that you need two views,

149
00:13:59,830 --> 00:14:03,590
two orthogonal views, to localize an abnormality.

150
00:14:03,850 --> 00:14:05,750
Now, there will be exceptions to this rule,

151
00:14:06,150 --> 00:14:09,350
and we'll talk about the silhouette sign in later modules.

152
00:14:09,750 --> 00:14:13,310
But again, remember that radiographs are two-dimensional representations

153
00:14:13,310 --> 00:14:17,430
of three-dimensional objects, and for true localization,

154
00:14:17,610 --> 00:14:20,450
you're going to need two views orthogonal to one another.

155
00:14:20,910 --> 00:14:26,270
As another example, a nodule in this location could potentially be located

156
00:14:26,270 --> 00:14:30,630
on the frontal view, either in the middle lobe, as seen here on the lateral view,

157
00:14:31,170 --> 00:14:34,090
or in the left lower lobe.

158
00:14:35,190 --> 00:14:39,130
Again, both of those producing the same appearance on the frontal view.

159
00:14:39,130 --> 00:14:46,780
I do want to talk about two exceptions where you can potentially localize the nodule

160
00:14:46,780 --> 00:14:48,680
on a frontal view alone.

161
00:14:49,880 --> 00:14:55,040
If we have a nodule way up at the lung apex, well above the aortic arch,

162
00:14:55,140 --> 00:14:58,780
we know that the lower lobe does not extend this far a cranial.

163
00:14:58,860 --> 00:15:00,840
So, if we see a nodule on the frontal view here,

164
00:15:00,900 --> 00:15:04,880
we can be certain it's in the right upper lobe.

165
00:15:05,720 --> 00:15:09,140
And then finally, way down in the lateral costafrenic sulcus,

166
00:15:09,140 --> 00:15:12,380
the middle lobe does not extend that far laterally.

167
00:15:12,740 --> 00:15:15,100
So, if we see something down in the sulcus here,

168
00:15:15,320 --> 00:15:19,020
we can be pretty confident that that is in the right lower lobe.

169
00:15:21,080 --> 00:15:23,400
Otherwise, in the absence of the silhouette sign,

170
00:15:23,480 --> 00:15:25,440
which we'll talk about a little later,

171
00:15:26,100 --> 00:15:30,100
it's going to be difficult to confirm the position of a nodule

172
00:15:30,100 --> 00:15:31,960
on a frontal view alone.

173
00:15:31,960 --> 00:15:37,740
In later modules, we're going to discuss what exactly is meant by this silhouette sign

174
00:15:37,740 --> 00:15:42,680
and how this will help us localize abnormalities such as pneumonia

175
00:15:42,680 --> 00:15:45,100
within the lobes of the lung.

176
00:15:45,980 --> 00:15:50,300
Here, I just want you to be aware of the fact that it's important to understand

177
00:15:50,300 --> 00:15:58,300
which specific lung lobe abuts which mediastinal and diaphragmatic surface

178
00:15:58,300 --> 00:16:02,840
so that you'll know what part of the lung is actually contributing to that interface.

179
00:16:02,840 --> 00:16:07,420
So, for example, we talk about the right paratracheal stripe

180
00:16:07,420 --> 00:16:11,620
that is formed by the right upper lobe

181
00:16:11,620 --> 00:16:15,180
making that interface with the mediastinum.

182
00:16:15,180 --> 00:16:18,260
The right atrium or right heart border

183
00:16:18,260 --> 00:16:23,220
is adjacent to the middle lobe of the right lung.

184
00:16:24,200 --> 00:16:29,220
The aortic arch, that interface is created by the left upper lobe.

185
00:16:30,700 --> 00:16:33,640
The lingula is essentially the middle lobe equivalent.

186
00:16:33,940 --> 00:16:36,740
It's that segment of the left upper lobe

187
00:16:36,740 --> 00:16:38,700
that abuts the left heart border.

188
00:16:39,940 --> 00:16:42,260
The descending thoracic aorta,

189
00:16:42,740 --> 00:16:44,880
that interface is created by the aorta

190
00:16:44,880 --> 00:16:46,380
and the left lower lobe.

191
00:16:47,580 --> 00:16:51,460
And then finally, the right and left portions of the diaphragm

192
00:16:51,460 --> 00:16:55,380
are adjacent to the lower lobes.

193
00:16:56,600 --> 00:17:01,960
On the posterior aspect of the heart,

194
00:17:02,080 --> 00:17:03,220
on the lateral view,

195
00:17:03,600 --> 00:17:06,040
that is created by the heart margin,

196
00:17:06,160 --> 00:17:07,760
the left atrium and left ventricle

197
00:17:07,760 --> 00:17:09,440
abutting the left lower lobe.

198
00:17:09,860 --> 00:17:12,980
So again, keep in mind what specific lobes

199
00:17:12,980 --> 00:17:16,320
create what interfaces on the frontal and lateral view

200
00:17:16,320 --> 00:17:20,220
and this will be helpful in localizing abnormalities

201
00:17:20,220 --> 00:17:22,260
when these specific interfaces are lost.

202
00:17:22,480 --> 00:17:25,840
And again, we'll talk about that in more detail in a later module.

203
00:17:27,420 --> 00:17:29,060
As I alluded to previously,

204
00:17:29,240 --> 00:17:31,300
the fissures may or may not be visible.

205
00:17:31,860 --> 00:17:34,280
This will depend on the particular view,

206
00:17:34,940 --> 00:17:37,240
whether we're talking about a frontal view or lateral view,

207
00:17:37,780 --> 00:17:39,200
what fissure we're talking about,

208
00:17:39,540 --> 00:17:42,860
and the orientation of the fissure relative to the x-ray beam

209
00:17:42,860 --> 00:17:44,020
in that particular patient.

210
00:17:44,720 --> 00:17:45,760
So in the frontal view,

211
00:17:46,440 --> 00:17:48,360
the minor fissure is really the only one

212
00:17:48,360 --> 00:17:50,100
that you're going to be able to identify.

213
00:17:50,700 --> 00:17:53,900
And the more horizontal this is oriented,

214
00:17:53,980 --> 00:17:56,780
the more likely you are to see that fissure.

215
00:17:56,780 --> 00:17:58,160
The oblique fissures,

216
00:17:58,520 --> 00:18:01,060
given their positioning and obliquity,

217
00:18:01,200 --> 00:18:03,280
are not typically identified on the frontal view.

218
00:18:03,840 --> 00:18:05,860
Now both the minor and major fissures

219
00:18:05,860 --> 00:18:09,380
are potentially visible on the lateral view,

220
00:18:09,460 --> 00:18:10,900
given their orientation.

221
00:18:12,140 --> 00:18:13,220
In this example,

222
00:18:13,980 --> 00:18:14,840
if you'll notice,

223
00:18:15,980 --> 00:18:18,400
we're able to see a thin line,

224
00:18:18,760 --> 00:18:19,720
obliquely oriented,

225
00:18:20,300 --> 00:18:23,040
and that corresponds to one of the oblique fissures,

226
00:18:23,580 --> 00:18:25,300
as we can see there.

227
00:18:26,240 --> 00:18:27,620
And I'll take off that line

228
00:18:27,620 --> 00:18:29,540
and then outline that with arrows.

229
00:18:30,440 --> 00:18:33,540
So hopefully you can make out that fissure.

230
00:18:33,540 --> 00:18:36,160
You can identify normal fissures.

231
00:18:36,640 --> 00:18:39,200
Sometimes the fissures become visible

232
00:18:39,200 --> 00:18:41,640
because they are thickened.

233
00:18:42,020 --> 00:18:44,540
They are highlighted by airspace disease

234
00:18:44,540 --> 00:18:46,300
on one side of the fissure,

235
00:18:46,600 --> 00:18:48,280
or they have fluid,

236
00:18:48,640 --> 00:18:51,020
which is collecting within the fissure,

237
00:18:51,100 --> 00:18:54,120
all of which can make the fissure visible.

238
00:18:55,520 --> 00:18:57,240
There are subtle differences

239
00:18:57,240 --> 00:18:59,880
between the positioning

240
00:18:59,880 --> 00:19:03,800
and steepness of the oblique fissures

241
00:19:03,800 --> 00:19:05,080
between when we're talking about

242
00:19:05,080 --> 00:19:06,320
distinguishing right and left,

243
00:19:06,420 --> 00:19:09,060
but that's beyond the scope of this discussion.

244
00:19:11,170 --> 00:19:12,390
Here are two examples

245
00:19:12,390 --> 00:19:14,390
in which a pathologic process

246
00:19:14,390 --> 00:19:16,070
renders the minor fissure

247
00:19:16,070 --> 00:19:18,470
visible on the frontal radiograph.

248
00:19:19,070 --> 00:19:20,630
On the case on the left,

249
00:19:21,090 --> 00:19:23,050
we have a little bit of fluid

250
00:19:23,050 --> 00:19:24,090
within the fissure

251
00:19:24,090 --> 00:19:26,130
and adjacent airspace disease.

252
00:19:26,130 --> 00:19:28,510
So we're able to see the minor fissure

253
00:19:28,510 --> 00:19:29,510
very easily.

254
00:19:30,430 --> 00:19:31,810
On the case on the right,

255
00:19:31,970 --> 00:19:34,170
we have right upper lobe airspace disease

256
00:19:34,170 --> 00:19:35,270
from pneumonia.

257
00:19:35,970 --> 00:19:37,230
That airspace disease

258
00:19:37,230 --> 00:19:38,650
stops at the minor fissure,

259
00:19:38,810 --> 00:19:40,150
so you're able to see the fissure

260
00:19:40,150 --> 00:19:41,090
as an interface

261
00:19:41,090 --> 00:19:43,430
between the airspace disease

262
00:19:43,430 --> 00:19:44,910
in the right upper lobe

263
00:19:44,910 --> 00:19:46,730
and then normal aeration

264
00:19:46,730 --> 00:19:48,350
or relative normal aeration

265
00:19:48,350 --> 00:19:50,490
in the right middle lobe.

266
00:19:50,490 --> 00:19:53,430
This example highlights

267
00:19:53,430 --> 00:19:54,570
why it is so important

268
00:19:54,570 --> 00:19:57,250
to understand normal anatomy

269
00:19:57,250 --> 00:19:59,630
and have a basic understanding

270
00:19:59,630 --> 00:20:01,250
of pathologic processes

271
00:20:01,250 --> 00:20:03,890
when interpreting chest radiography.

272
00:20:04,350 --> 00:20:06,050
So you look at the frontal view

273
00:20:06,050 --> 00:20:09,030
in the right midlung,

274
00:20:09,750 --> 00:20:10,650
I think you can make out

275
00:20:10,650 --> 00:20:12,690
two opacities which appear

276
00:20:12,690 --> 00:20:13,990
to be pulmonary nodules.

277
00:20:14,530 --> 00:20:15,990
However, if we correlate that

278
00:20:15,990 --> 00:20:18,010
with the lateral chest radiograph,

279
00:20:18,010 --> 00:20:19,990
hopefully you can identify

280
00:20:19,990 --> 00:20:21,890
those opacities

281
00:20:21,890 --> 00:20:24,250
as kind of running obliquely

282
00:20:24,250 --> 00:20:25,830
in the expected position

283
00:20:25,830 --> 00:20:27,750
of the right major

284
00:20:27,750 --> 00:20:28,890
or oblique fissure.

285
00:20:29,530 --> 00:20:30,370
And you'll notice

286
00:20:30,370 --> 00:20:31,430
that those opacities

287
00:20:31,430 --> 00:20:32,470
are distributed

288
00:20:32,470 --> 00:20:33,890
on either side

289
00:20:33,890 --> 00:20:35,150
of that fissure

290
00:20:35,150 --> 00:20:36,570
as we can see here

291
00:20:36,570 --> 00:20:38,290
and here.

292
00:20:39,050 --> 00:20:40,510
And so this actually represents

293
00:20:40,510 --> 00:20:42,150
fluid loculated

294
00:20:42,150 --> 00:20:44,770
within the right oblique

295
00:20:44,770 --> 00:20:45,770
or major fissure

296
00:20:45,770 --> 00:20:46,850
in a patient

297
00:20:46,850 --> 00:20:48,450
that has pulmonary edema.

298
00:20:48,850 --> 00:20:49,530
So these are not

299
00:20:49,530 --> 00:20:50,410
pulmonary nodules

300
00:20:50,410 --> 00:20:52,090
but instead fluid

301
00:20:52,090 --> 00:20:53,330
located within

302
00:20:53,330 --> 00:20:54,950
the right oblique fissure.

303
00:20:56,090 --> 00:20:57,570
Here is an example

304
00:20:57,570 --> 00:20:59,370
of more confluent

305
00:20:59,370 --> 00:21:01,510
or consolidative pneumonia

306
00:21:01,510 --> 00:21:03,870
rendering the right lung

307
00:21:03,870 --> 00:21:05,190
fissures visible.

308
00:21:06,250 --> 00:21:07,890
On the frontal view,

309
00:21:08,130 --> 00:21:09,110
which is on our left,

310
00:21:09,730 --> 00:21:10,490
we're able to see

311
00:21:10,490 --> 00:21:11,210
the interface

312
00:21:11,210 --> 00:21:13,110
between the right upper lobe

313
00:21:13,110 --> 00:21:14,210
airspace disease

314
00:21:14,210 --> 00:21:15,770
and the aerated

315
00:21:15,770 --> 00:21:16,850
right middle lobe.

316
00:21:17,290 --> 00:21:19,370
That interface represents

317
00:21:19,370 --> 00:21:20,450
the minor fissure.

318
00:21:21,370 --> 00:21:22,970
On the lateral view,

319
00:21:23,130 --> 00:21:23,830
we're able to see

320
00:21:23,830 --> 00:21:25,550
two interfaces.

321
00:21:26,390 --> 00:21:27,510
The first interface

322
00:21:27,510 --> 00:21:28,410
as shown here

323
00:21:28,410 --> 00:21:29,830
is the interface

324
00:21:29,830 --> 00:21:31,390
along the major

325
00:21:31,390 --> 00:21:32,470
or oblique fissure

326
00:21:32,470 --> 00:21:34,430
between the right upper lobe

327
00:21:34,430 --> 00:21:35,130
and the adjacent

328
00:21:35,130 --> 00:21:36,950
aerated right lower lobe.

329
00:21:37,890 --> 00:21:38,650
And then finally,

330
00:21:38,770 --> 00:21:39,370
we're going to have

331
00:21:39,370 --> 00:21:40,810
that other interface

332
00:21:40,810 --> 00:21:42,250
between the aerated

333
00:21:42,250 --> 00:21:42,970
middle lobe

334
00:21:42,970 --> 00:21:44,990
and the opaque

335
00:21:44,990 --> 00:21:46,610
right upper lobe

336
00:21:46,610 --> 00:21:48,410
given a right upper lobe

337
00:21:48,410 --> 00:21:48,930
pneumonia.

338
00:21:50,130 --> 00:21:51,930
So this is an example

339
00:21:51,930 --> 00:21:53,090
of a typical pneumonia

340
00:21:53,090 --> 00:21:54,370
which respects

341
00:21:55,030 --> 00:21:56,290
fissural boundaries

342
00:21:56,290 --> 00:21:57,830
and does not cross

343
00:21:57,830 --> 00:21:58,670
that fissure.

344
00:22:00,830 --> 00:22:02,270
Using cross-sectional

345
00:22:02,270 --> 00:22:03,190
anatomy again

346
00:22:03,190 --> 00:22:04,870
to understand

347
00:22:04,870 --> 00:22:06,390
these radiographic findings,

348
00:22:06,390 --> 00:22:07,650
on the left,

349
00:22:08,290 --> 00:22:09,690
we have the right upper lobe

350
00:22:09,690 --> 00:22:10,130
pneumonia

351
00:22:10,130 --> 00:22:12,170
on the lateral view.

352
00:22:12,550 --> 00:22:13,590
We're able to identify

353
00:22:13,590 --> 00:22:14,510
both the horizontal

354
00:22:14,510 --> 00:22:15,450
or minor fissure

355
00:22:15,450 --> 00:22:17,110
and the oblique

356
00:22:17,110 --> 00:22:17,990
or major fissure.

357
00:22:19,110 --> 00:22:19,830
There's that

358
00:22:19,830 --> 00:22:20,950
on the right side

359
00:22:20,950 --> 00:22:22,330
the reconstructed

360
00:22:22,330 --> 00:22:22,990
sagittal view

361
00:22:22,990 --> 00:22:24,010
of the left lung

362
00:22:24,010 --> 00:22:25,430
where we can see

363
00:22:25,430 --> 00:22:26,890
a portion of the oblique fissure

364
00:22:26,890 --> 00:22:28,730
labeled with the yellow arrow

365
00:22:28,730 --> 00:22:30,010
and then the minor

366
00:22:30,010 --> 00:22:31,650
or horizontal fissure

367
00:22:31,650 --> 00:22:33,370
labeled also with a yellow arrow.

368
00:22:33,370 --> 00:22:36,190
Notice that if we have

369
00:22:36,190 --> 00:22:38,270
a consolidative

370
00:22:38,270 --> 00:22:39,430
airspace process

371
00:22:39,430 --> 00:22:40,450
in the right upper lobe

372
00:22:40,450 --> 00:22:41,290
as shown here,

373
00:22:41,930 --> 00:22:42,910
we're going to be able

374
00:22:42,910 --> 00:22:44,510
to identify the interfaces

375
00:22:44,510 --> 00:22:46,850
between this airspace

376
00:22:46,850 --> 00:22:48,630
opacity in the right upper lobe

377
00:22:48,630 --> 00:22:50,570
with the aerated lung

378
00:22:50,570 --> 00:22:51,650
of the middle lobe

379
00:22:51,650 --> 00:22:53,030
at the horizontal fissure

380
00:22:53,030 --> 00:22:54,570
and the lower lobe

381
00:22:54,570 --> 00:22:56,310
at the oblique fissure interface.

382
00:22:56,990 --> 00:22:58,630
There are several

383
00:22:58,630 --> 00:22:59,710
accessory fissures

384
00:22:59,710 --> 00:23:00,810
that you may encounter

385
00:23:00,810 --> 00:23:01,910
as normal variants

386
00:23:01,910 --> 00:23:03,610
on radiography.

387
00:23:04,270 --> 00:23:05,330
The most common fissure

388
00:23:05,330 --> 00:23:06,610
will be the azagous fissure.

389
00:23:07,570 --> 00:23:08,350
This represents

390
00:23:08,350 --> 00:23:09,390
a normal variant

391
00:23:09,390 --> 00:23:10,150
that's created

392
00:23:10,150 --> 00:23:11,430
by a laterally displaced

393
00:23:11,430 --> 00:23:12,310
azagous vein

394
00:23:12,310 --> 00:23:14,050
that invaginates

395
00:23:14,050 --> 00:23:15,870
down through the pleura

396
00:23:15,870 --> 00:23:18,010
and creates this deep fissure

397
00:23:18,010 --> 00:23:19,830
in the apical segment

398
00:23:19,830 --> 00:23:20,990
of the right upper lobe.

399
00:23:22,030 --> 00:23:22,950
The image on the right

400
00:23:22,950 --> 00:23:23,910
is a CT

401
00:23:23,910 --> 00:23:26,110
at the right apex

402
00:23:26,110 --> 00:23:28,570
showing the fissure

403
00:23:28,570 --> 00:23:30,410
and then the medially located

404
00:23:30,410 --> 00:23:31,510
azagous lobe.

405
00:23:31,910 --> 00:23:33,450
On radiography

406
00:23:33,450 --> 00:23:34,690
this manifests itself

407
00:23:34,690 --> 00:23:37,310
as an obliquely oriented line

408
00:23:37,310 --> 00:23:38,410
primarily vertical

409
00:23:38,410 --> 00:23:41,970
in the medial right lung apex

410
00:23:41,970 --> 00:23:44,170
as is seen here.

411
00:23:51,800 --> 00:23:53,040
This question tests

412
00:23:53,040 --> 00:23:53,660
your ability

413
00:23:53,660 --> 00:23:55,140
to localize the pneumonia

414
00:23:55,140 --> 00:23:57,400
based on normal anatomy

415
00:23:57,400 --> 00:23:58,380
of the lung lobes

416
00:23:58,380 --> 00:23:58,940
and fissures.

417
00:23:59,820 --> 00:24:00,680
On the frontal view

418
00:24:00,680 --> 00:24:01,760
there is a subtle

419
00:24:01,760 --> 00:24:03,780
generalized increased density

420
00:24:03,780 --> 00:24:05,300
in the left lung

421
00:24:05,300 --> 00:24:06,860
that localizes

422
00:24:06,860 --> 00:24:08,460
anterior to the major

423
00:24:08,460 --> 00:24:09,640
or oblique fissure

424
00:24:09,640 --> 00:24:10,940
on the lateral view.

425
00:24:11,980 --> 00:24:13,060
Therefore this corresponds

426
00:24:13,060 --> 00:24:14,840
to the left upper lobe

427
00:24:14,840 --> 00:24:16,300
as it is anterior

428
00:24:16,300 --> 00:24:18,460
to the major fissure.

429
00:24:19,260 --> 00:24:21,000
Note that on the lateral view

430
00:24:21,000 --> 00:24:23,020
the major or oblique fissure

431
00:24:23,020 --> 00:24:24,360
is identified

432
00:24:24,360 --> 00:24:25,600
as the interface

433
00:24:25,600 --> 00:24:28,380
between the left upper lobe

434
00:24:28,380 --> 00:24:28,920
pneumonia

435
00:24:28,920 --> 00:24:31,580
or airspace disease

436
00:24:31,580 --> 00:24:33,180
and the normally aerated

437
00:24:33,180 --> 00:24:34,780
left lower lobe.

438
00:24:37,170 --> 00:24:37,950
To summarize

439
00:24:37,950 --> 00:24:39,250
we've now completed

440
00:24:39,250 --> 00:24:39,950
our tour

441
00:24:39,950 --> 00:24:41,150
of the normal anatomy

442
00:24:41,150 --> 00:24:42,770
of the thorax

443
00:24:42,770 --> 00:24:43,550
on both the frontal

444
00:24:43,550 --> 00:24:44,570
and lateral views.

445
00:24:44,570 --> 00:24:46,530
we looked specifically

446
00:24:46,530 --> 00:24:48,190
at what constitutes

447
00:24:48,190 --> 00:24:49,130
the borders

448
00:24:49,130 --> 00:24:50,110
of the mediastinum

449
00:24:50,110 --> 00:24:51,290
on both of these views.

450
00:24:52,170 --> 00:24:52,990
We talked about

451
00:24:52,990 --> 00:24:54,450
some clinically important

452
00:24:54,450 --> 00:24:55,970
and often encountered

453
00:24:55,970 --> 00:24:57,370
stripes and spaces

454
00:24:57,370 --> 00:24:58,850
on these views.

455
00:24:59,490 --> 00:25:00,270
So for example

456
00:25:00,270 --> 00:25:00,890
we looked at

457
00:25:00,890 --> 00:25:02,610
the right paratracheal stripe

458
00:25:02,610 --> 00:25:04,290
and left paraspinal stripe

459
00:25:04,290 --> 00:25:05,730
on the frontal view

460
00:25:05,730 --> 00:25:07,450
as well as the posterior

461
00:25:07,450 --> 00:25:08,390
or tracheal stripe

462
00:25:08,390 --> 00:25:09,250
on the lateral view.

463
00:25:10,190 --> 00:25:10,750
We talked about

464
00:25:10,750 --> 00:25:11,730
important spaces

465
00:25:11,730 --> 00:25:12,470
such as the

466
00:25:12,470 --> 00:25:13,730
aortopulmonary window

467
00:25:13,730 --> 00:25:16,370
and the azigoesophageal recess

468
00:25:16,370 --> 00:25:17,630
on the frontal view.

469
00:25:18,350 --> 00:25:19,170
On the lateral view

470
00:25:19,170 --> 00:25:19,950
we concentrated

471
00:25:19,950 --> 00:25:22,590
on the retrotracheal triangle

472
00:25:22,590 --> 00:25:23,690
as well as the

473
00:25:23,690 --> 00:25:24,350
retrosternal

474
00:25:24,350 --> 00:25:25,790
and retrocardiac spaces.

475
00:25:26,490 --> 00:25:27,650
We also talked about

476
00:25:27,650 --> 00:25:28,430
the normal

477
00:25:28,430 --> 00:25:30,450
increase in

478
00:25:30,450 --> 00:25:31,290
radiolucency

479
00:25:31,290 --> 00:25:32,090
over the spine

480
00:25:32,090 --> 00:25:32,670
as you move

481
00:25:32,670 --> 00:25:33,610
from cranial

482
00:25:33,610 --> 00:25:34,690
to caudal.

483
00:25:35,590 --> 00:25:36,090
We looked at

484
00:25:36,090 --> 00:25:37,370
the major airways

485
00:25:37,370 --> 00:25:38,330
on both the frontal

486
00:25:38,330 --> 00:25:39,370
and lateral views.

487
00:25:40,390 --> 00:25:40,970
We looked at

488
00:25:40,970 --> 00:25:41,330
the

489
00:25:41,330 --> 00:25:43,590
fissural anatomy

490
00:25:43,590 --> 00:25:44,650
and how these

491
00:25:44,650 --> 00:25:45,270
contribute to

492
00:25:45,270 --> 00:25:45,650
the different

493
00:25:45,650 --> 00:25:46,370
lung lobes

494
00:25:46,370 --> 00:25:46,990
on both the

495
00:25:46,990 --> 00:25:47,490
right and the

496
00:25:47,490 --> 00:25:47,750
left

497
00:25:47,750 --> 00:25:48,410
and how that

498
00:25:48,410 --> 00:25:49,630
anatomy varies

499
00:25:49,630 --> 00:25:50,570
between the

500
00:25:50,570 --> 00:25:51,150
right and the

501
00:25:51,150 --> 00:25:51,630
left lung.

502
00:25:52,550 --> 00:25:53,050
We talked about

503
00:25:53,050 --> 00:25:53,950
the diaphragm

504
00:25:53,950 --> 00:25:54,690
on both the

505
00:25:54,690 --> 00:25:55,410
frontal and lateral

506
00:25:55,410 --> 00:25:55,990
views.

507
00:25:56,870 --> 00:25:57,790
Different ways

508
00:25:57,790 --> 00:25:58,430
to distinguish

509
00:25:58,430 --> 00:25:59,170
the right and

510
00:25:59,170 --> 00:25:59,730
left portions

511
00:25:59,730 --> 00:26:00,510
of the diaphragm

512
00:26:00,510 --> 00:26:01,050
on the lateral

513
00:26:01,050 --> 00:26:01,430
view.

514
00:26:01,990 --> 00:26:02,610
Then we talked

515
00:26:02,610 --> 00:26:03,030
about the

516
00:26:03,030 --> 00:26:03,710
importance

517
00:26:03,710 --> 00:26:05,170
of evaluating

518
00:26:05,170 --> 00:26:06,070
the sharpness

519
00:26:06,070 --> 00:26:07,150
of the

520
00:26:07,150 --> 00:26:08,470
costophrinic angles

521
00:26:08,470 --> 00:26:09,150
or sulci

522
00:26:09,150 --> 00:26:10,570
on the frontal

523
00:26:10,570 --> 00:26:11,170
view which is

524
00:26:11,170 --> 00:26:11,710
the lateral

525
00:26:11,710 --> 00:26:12,350
costophrinic

526
00:26:12,350 --> 00:26:12,730
sulcus

527
00:26:12,730 --> 00:26:13,990
and the

528
00:26:13,990 --> 00:26:14,890
lateral view

529
00:26:14,890 --> 00:26:15,490
which is the

530
00:26:15,490 --> 00:26:15,870
posterior

531
00:26:15,870 --> 00:26:16,590
costophrinic

532
00:26:16,590 --> 00:26:17,010
sulcus.

533
00:26:17,630 --> 00:26:18,270
Then finally

534
00:26:18,270 --> 00:26:19,130
